search(p) {  // first index not strictly better than p
  const a = this.px; let lo = 0, hi = a.length;
  if (this.desc) while (lo < hi) { const m = (lo + hi) >> 1; if (a[m] > p) lo = m + 1; else hi = m; }
  else while (lo < hi) { const m = (lo + hi) >> 1; if (a[m] < p) lo = m + 1; else hi = m; }
  return lo;
}
```
set(p, q): if q is 0, splice the level out when it exists (deleting a missing level is a no op); if the price exists, overwrite qty; otherwise splice in at the insertion point. Search is O(log n), about 7 comparisons at n = 100; insert and delete are O(n) because splice shifts up to 100 numbers, a few hundred bytes of contiguous memory. Best bid and ask are index 0, O(1); top(10) for the checksum is O(10); truncate is O(1) by setting length. A randomized test of 400 inserts, updates and deletes checks strict ordering on both sides.
